they had come in their thousands  once again calling for prime minister  prayut chanocha to resign  the protesters vowed to remain until  their demands were met  the next morning the government hit back  it arrested opposition leaders  and removed demonstrators camped out in  front of the prime minister's office  it also banned gatherings of more than  five people  and the publication of news it deems a  security threat  the anti-government rallies started in  july apart from fresh elections and a  new constitution  the opposition also wants to limit the  powers of the king  which has not gone down well with thai  royalists  i think the royalist supporters are out  here because they love the country  we love the country as well i'm not  worried about violence because both  sides are doing what they think is best  for the country  but scuffles did break out between  opponents and supporters of the monarchy  thailand's royal family is backed by the  military and traditionally revered by  the population  but king maha what shira longhorn is  less popular than his father  who died in 2016. the king's motorcade  met with chance in a three-fingered  salute  unthinkable in earlier times the gesture  has become a pro-democracy symbol  borrowed from the hunger games film  series about a totalitarian regime  harsh laws have stifled public criticism  of the monarchy  but the king has stirred dissent with  his extravagant lifestyle  and long holidays abroad during the  pandemic  opposition leader anon nampa called out  the king on stage during the protests  our king does not reside in thailand i'd  like to ask directly  what's so bad about living in thailand  he was later arrested with the anger  boiling over  the government is keen to put a lid on  the protests any way it can  well for more now i'm joined by  professor titi nan  pong sudhirak at chula longhorn  university in bangkok  professor protests are nothing new in  thailand but  these seem different what's driving  people to take to the streets  this time so persistently  well if you follow thai politics you  know you've seen um street  demonstrations and  protests and polarizations over the last  15 years  punctuated by two military crews in 2006  2014  two new constitutions in 2007-2017  this time the protests are being led by  a different generation  and they're making different demands and  this is a younger generation younger  voices basically people  under their 40s i mean people in their  20s and 30s  they have seen over the last 15 years  that you know they they have no future  because thailand is at a standstill  time has been set by nepotism corruption  economic risk management  no growth strategy going forward so for  them these younger  people younger demographics they want to  reclaim  their future for jobs for livelihoods  and so on  they're also making a different kind of  demands for thailand it's a  profoundly new set of demands to to call  for  you know the prime minister's  resignation we've seen that kind of  demand before but  a new constitution in order to reset to  recalibrate we make thai democracy into  a genuine democratic system  and the most profound of all that we've  not heard of before is unprecedented is  the reform of the monarchy  and these three demands a tall order for  thailand  yet you know these young people are not  gonna go away because they they want  their future back they want time to move  ahead  so we're seeing a lot of tension now  because they're not being  accommodated there's no concession no  compromise only a crackdown  you mentioned the monarchy kings are  traditionally revered  in thai society why have the protesters  turned against the monarchy and  how significant is this  well you know if you if you look at  thailand from outside  you have to understand that uh thailand  as we know it today and this is a  significant country 17 million people  population  20th most populous country in the world  as a  sizable gdp is a you know main country  in  the hub of mainland southeast asia so  it's an important country  and this country has been known all of  the  all what we know about thailand comes  from the one rain under the  the throne and the reign of king rama  the ninth king  the laking and now there's a new monarch  so it's a new era  the reason people are so uh you know  respectful and they find the king the  laking so popular is because  we went through communist threat under  the communist threat so we had this uh  all around thailand you can see you know  indochina  burma and so on a lot the neighborhood  had a hard time but thailand  persevered and got through the communist  era at the same time thailand had  economic development and the laking  devoted himself  to the betterment of the country for  china's development so people  respected that they appreciated that but  now with the new era i think a lot of  people also think that thailand needs to  change to reform  in order to move to the 21st century in  order to arrive in the 21st century  because they all institutions of the  monarchy the military the bureaucracy  judiciary don't work the same way as  they did before  okay let's get back to the crackdown  that the government has  launched now against the protesters do  you think that that crack town is likely  to stop  these protests  in the short term you know this is a  very  anxious 10th day in bangkok because the  crackdown comes with a  a severe imposition of emergency  decree which means it has empowered  the authorities to arrest detain people  there's a kind of a suppression taking  place now  certainly in the short term this means  that the protest movement  will have a we'll face a setback because  the leaders will be arrested  and there'll be more arrests i'm sure  some imprisonment  but in a longer term you know it's not  about the protesters and the protests  it's about the grievances that are  backing them that are behind them  so unless these businesses are addressed  somehow  we will see them back for long professor  thank you very  much we really appreciate your insights  on this complex story that was professor  titinan pong sudirak  thank you very much thank you  you

